// Stories for the matrix: STORIES["<civ id>:<activity>"] = "…". See cultures/README.md.
Object.assign(STORIES, {
  // ── Hindustanis ──
  "hindustanis:hunt": "In the Mughal shikargah the emperor Akbar ordered the qamargha, a great ring hunt: thousands of beaters closed a circle many miles wide over days, driving deer, nilgai and leopards toward the centre. In 1578 near Bhera, at the height of such a hunt, Akbar suddenly halted it, freed the animals and sat in silence under a tree. Courtiers called it a spiritual seizure. The hunt was power; the release was something deeper.",
  "hindustanis:battle": "Panipat, 21 April 1526. Babur, with barely twelve thousand men, lashed carts together with chains and set his matchlocks and cannon behind them. Ibrahim Lodi came on with tens of thousands and a thousand elephants. The guns roared, the elephants panicked and trampled their own ranks, and by noon the sultan lay dead on the field. Babur wrote in his memoir that God had made the hard easy. A new empire, the Mughal, began in smoke.",
  "hindustanis:gather": "Imagined: in the Doab between the Ganga and the Yamuna, women of a village go out at dawn after the rabi harvest to glean wheat ears and gather mahua flowers fallen in the night. Baskets on their heads, they sing kajri and bicker over who found the sweetest tree. The mahua will be dried, cooked, brewed. Little is wasted; the land between two rivers has fed them for three thousand years, and they expect it to keep doing so.",
  "hindustanis:ritual": "At Varanasi the pandas sit on the ghats beside the Ganga with palm-leaf almanacs, reading the stars for those who come. A merchant asks about a journey; a father about his daughter's marriage. The priest consults the nakshatra, names an auspicious muhurat, and tells him to offer flowers and a lamp to the river. As dusk falls the Ganga aarti begins, brass lamps swung in great arcs. No one is sad here; the future is being asked for, politely.",
  "hindustanis:rain": "By June the plains of Hindustan bake at forty-five degrees; wells sink, cattle stand panting under neem trees. Everyone watches the southwest sky. Then the first clouds of the monsoon pile up, the smell of wet earth, mitti ki khushboo, rises before the rain itself, and the downpour hits. Children dance in the lanes, women hang swings from mango trees for the festival of Teej and sing sawan songs. The whole country exhales at once.",
  "hindustanis:flood": "The Yamuna and the Ganga do not stay in their beds. In monsoon years the rivers spread for miles across the Doab, carrying away mud houses and leaving villages as islands. Families climb onto roofs with their goats and grain, waiting for the water to fall. Yet the farmers know the flood brings new silt; the field drowned in Bhadon will give a rich crop in winter. They mourn the loss and count the gift together.",
  "hindustanis:wind": "Before the monsoon comes the andhi, a dust storm that rolls across the Gangetic plain as a brown wall hundreds of metres high. The sky turns copper, then dark at midday. Shopkeepers in Delhi and Agra slam their shutters, camels kneel and close their eyes, and the dust finds its way into every jar and fold of cloth. When it passes, the air is cooler, and people say the rain is near now.",
  "hindustanis:fire": "Delhi, 1857. When the sepoy uprising reached the city, the Mughal capital burned in places as rebels and British forces fought street by street. The aged emperor Bahadur Shah Zafar, a poet more than a ruler, watched his city die. After the British retook Delhi, much of the old town was cleared and his line was ended. In exile in Rangoon he wrote that he could not find even two yards of ground in his beloved land for his grave.",
  "hindustanis:thunder": "In the songs of Hindustan, thunder is the voice of Indra riding his elephant Airavata through the clouds, hurling the vajra. When the first thunder of the monsoon cracks over the plains, peacocks scream in answer and lovers apart are said to ache, a theme poets from Kalidasa to Tansen returned to again and again. The raga Megh Malhar, it was said, could call down rain itself. The thunder is both fear and promise.",
  "hindustanis:birth": "In a Mughal palace at Fatehpur Sikri in 1569, the son long prayed for was born to Akbar after he walked barefoot to the Sufi Salim Chishti for a blessing. He named the boy Salim; he would become Jahangir. In village homes too, a birth is announced with a brass plate beaten and sweets handed out, and the elders wonder aloud what this child will be. Mothers die easily; hope runs anyway.",
  "hindustanis:death": "In Hindustan a death is met quickly. The body is washed, wrapped in white, laid on a bamboo bier, and carried to the burning ghat while the men chant Ram naam satya hai, the name of God is truth. The eldest son lights the pyre and later cracks the skull so the soul may leave. Thirteen days of mourning follow, and the ashes go to the Ganga, the river that carries everyone home.",

  // ── Indians ──
  "indians:hunt": "Imagined: in a Rajput court of Mewar, the prince rides out for shikar with cheetahs hooded on bullock carts, as painters of the 18th century often showed. When blackbuck are sighted, the hood is lifted and the cheetah streaks across the plain. The hunt is a lesson in courage and patience, and afterward court artists record it in bright miniatures. For the Rajputs, the jungle was a school for war.",
  "indians:battle": "Kalinga, around 261 BCE. The Maurya emperor Ashoka conquered the kingdom on the eastern coast in a war so bloody that his own edicts record a hundred thousand slain and a hundred and fifty thousand carried away. Then Ashoka did something no conqueror had done: he publicly confessed remorse, carving it in stone, and turned to the dhamma of the Buddha. The battle he won became the battle he regretted for the rest of his life.",
  "indians:gather": "Imagined: in the Western Ghats, women of a forest village gather honey, wild pepper and cardamom pods, singing to each other so as not to surprise an elephant. The pepper vine climbs every tree; for two thousand years it has drawn Roman, Arab and Portuguese ships to the Malabar coast. These women do not think of empires. They think of the season, the bees, and getting home before the rain.",
  "indians:ritual": "Vedic sacrifice: priests build the fire altar of bricks laid in exact geometry, the shulba sutras giving its measurements. The hotr chants the hymns of the Rig Veda, the adhvaryu pours ghee into Agni's flames, and the fire carries the offering to the gods. Later, astrologers drew up a child's horoscope from the jyotisha texts to ask what lay ahead. The ritual is precise, solemn, and confident: the cosmos listens to correct words.",
  "indians:rain": "On the Kerala coast around the first of June, the whole of India waits for the monsoon to break, and the weather office still announces it like a national event. Farmers in Maharashtra and Punjab watch the forecasts; if it is late, prices rise and prayers rise with them. When it comes, the land turns green in a week, schoolchildren float paper boats, and the country, in a sense, begins its year again.",
  "indians:flood": "Imagined: on the Brahmaputra in Assam the river swells every monsoon until it is miles wide. The Mising people build their houses on stilts, chang ghar, and keep boats tied to the doorposts. When the water rises they simply move upstairs with the hens and wait. Some years the flood takes everything; other years it only visits. They live with the river, not against it, as their grandparents did.",
  "indians:wind": "In October 1999 a super cyclone struck the coast of Odisha with winds above 250 km/h and a storm surge that drove seawater miles inland. Around ten thousand people died. Odisha remembered. When Cyclone Phailin came in 2013, the state evacuated nearly a million people into shelters, and the death toll fell to a few dozen. Grief had been turned into preparation, and the coast counts that as a victory.",
  "indians:fire": "Holika Dahan: on the night before Holi, villages and city lanes across India light great bonfires of wood and cow-dung cakes. The story tells of the demoness Holika who sat in fire holding the boy Prahlad, but the fire spared the devotee and burned her. People circle the flames, roast fresh grain, and the next morning throw coloured powder at one another. Here fire is not destruction but cleansing.",
  "indians:thunder": "Indra, king of the gods in the Rig Veda, slew the serpent Vritra with his thunderbolt and freed the waters to flow. Nearly a quarter of the hymns are addressed to him. When thunder rolls over the plains, the old meaning still sits behind it: the drought-demon has been struck and the rivers are released. Farmers look up at the sound and smile, because thunder means the waters are coming.",
  "indians:birth": "The samskaras begin before birth with ceremonies for the mother. When the child arrives, the father whispers into its ear and touches honey and ghee to its lips in the jatakarma rite; on the twelfth day comes the namakarana, the naming. The astrologer casts the horoscope and everyone leans in to hear it. Childbirth was dangerous and many did not survive, but every newborn might become a king, a saint, or a poet.",
  "indians:death": "The antyesti, the last sacrifice. The body is bathed and dressed, a lamp is lit by its head, and the family carries it to the cremation ground, the shmashana. The eldest son circles the pyre with fire and lights it. On the third day the bones are collected; on the tenth or thirteenth the shraddha rites feed the departed soul. Grief is real, but the teaching is calm: the self does not die; it only changes clothes.",

  // ── Bengalis ──
  "bengalis:hunt": "In the Sundarbans, the mangrove forest of the Ganges delta, honey-gatherers called mouals and woodcutters go in by boat, and the tigers hunt them. Before entering they pray to Bonbibi, the forest goddess honoured by Hindus and Muslims alike, who protects the poor against the tiger-demon Dakshin Rai. The men wear masks on the backs of their heads to confuse the tigers. Here the hunter is often the hunted.",
  "bengalis:battle": "Plassey, 23 June 1757. Siraj ud-Daulah, the young Nawab of Bengal, met Robert Clive's small East India Company force in a mango grove on the Bhagirathi. A rainstorm soaked the Nawab's powder while the British kept theirs dry under tarpaulins, and Mir Jafar, his commander, held his troops back by secret agreement. The Nawab fled and was killed days later. Bengal remembers the betrayal more than the battle.",
  "bengalis:gather": "Imagined: in the low country of Bengal after the aman rice harvest, families wade into the ponds to gather shapla water lilies and catch small fish by hand, while children collect date-palm sap for jaggery in the cold mornings. Nobanna, the festival of new rice, follows with pitha cakes and songs. In a land of water, gathering means a basket, a net and wet ankles, and the mood is generous.",
  "bengalis:ritual": "Durga Puja in autumn: for five days Bengal worships the goddess Durga, who slew the buffalo-demon Mahishasura. Clay images, shaped by potters of Kumartuli in Kolkata, stand in pandals. Drummers play the dhak, women offer lamps, and the priest chants Sanskrit verses. People ask the goddess for protection in the year ahead. On the last day she is carried to the river and immersed, and the crowds say: come again next year.",
  "bengalis:rain": "Rabindranath Tagore wrote dozens of songs for the barsha, the monsoon, and Bengal still sings them when the clouds come. After the furnace of Boishakh, the first heavy rain falls on tin roofs and banana leaves, the ponds fill, frogs start up their chorus, and fishermen grin. Poets sit by windows with tea and muri. In Bengal the rains are not just weather; they are the season of longing and of song.",
  "bengalis:flood": "In 1998 two-thirds of Bangladesh lay underwater for more than two months as the Ganges, Brahmaputra and Meghna rose together. Millions lost homes and crops. Yet the delta itself is built by floods: every year the rivers lay down fresh silt that keeps Bengal among the most fertile places on earth. Villagers raise their homes on earthen plinths and keep a boat ready. Their word for the good flood, barsha, differs from the bad one, bonna.",
  "bengalis:wind": "On 12 November 1970 the Bhola cyclone struck East Pakistan, now Bangladesh, with a storm surge that swept over the low islands of the delta at night. Perhaps three hundred thousand people died, the deadliest tropical cyclone ever recorded. The government's slow response fuelled the anger that led to independence the next year. Today Bangladesh's cyclone shelters and volunteer warning teams have cut storm deaths enormously, a hard-won pride.",
  "bengalis:fire": "Imagined: in a village near Bishnupur, the terracotta temple town, potters fire their kilns at night before the Durga Puja season. The whole family tends the flames, feeding straw and dung cakes, and children are told stories of the gods while the clay hardens into horses and goddesses. A cracked pot is a small grief; a good firing means food for a month. The fire glows red over the fields.",
  "bengalis:thunder": "Kalbaishakhi: the nor'westers of spring. In the afternoons of April and May, black clouds rise suddenly in the northwest, and within minutes a storm of thunder, lightning and hail tears across Bengal. Mango crops are lost, boats overturned on the rivers, and lightning kills farmers in the open fields. Yet the storms also break the heat, and Bengalis welcome them with a mix of fear and relief, as the year's wild herald.",
  "bengalis:birth": "Imagined: in a village in the delta, the women ring the conch three times and ululate, ulu dhvani, to announce that a child has been born. The midwife, the dai, has done this a hundred times and lost some. The grandmother marks the baby with a smudge of kajal to keep away the evil eye. On the sixth night, Shashthi, the goddess of children, is honoured, for the fate of the child is said to be written that night.",
  "bengalis:death": "At Nimtala ghat in Kolkata on the Hooghly, the dead have been burned for centuries; Tagore himself was cremated there in 1941 amid vast crowds. Mourners chant Bolo Hari, Hari bol, as they carry the bier through the streets. The son performs mukhagni, touching fire to the mouth, and the river receives the ashes. Bengali Muslims, meanwhile, bury their dead quickly beneath a simple mound, and recite the janaza prayer.",

  // ── Dravidians ──
  "dravidians:hunt": "The Sangam poems of old Tamilakam, written in the first centuries CE, divide the land into five tinai. In kurinji, the hills, men hunted boar and elephant with bows and trapped deer, while girls guarded the millet fields from parrots with slings and rattles. Lovers met in the hills by night. The hunters sang to Murugan, the young red god of the mountains, and offered him a share of the kill.",
  "dravidians:battle": "Imagined, after the Sangam poems: before a war, Tamil warriors of the Chera, Chola and Pandya kings drank toddy, raised the war drum, and swore loyalty to their lord. Those who fell were honoured with a nadukal, a hero stone set up on the field, carved with their image, and worshipped by their village. Death in battle was not a waste but the finest end a man could have, said the puram poems.",
  "dravidians:gather": "On the Coromandel coast at Kayalpattinam and Tuticorin, pearl divers of the Paravar community dove for oysters in the Gulf of Mannar, a fishery known to Greek and Roman merchants. Holding a stone to sink fast, they went down without breath for a minute at a time, while shark-charmers chanted on the boats. Most oysters held nothing. A few held pearls that ended up in the crowns of kings across the world.",
  "dravidians:ritual": "At the great Brihadeeswara temple in Thanjavur, built by Rajaraja Chola around 1010, hundreds of temple servants, musicians and dancers tended the god Shiva. Inscriptions name them. Before a voyage or a war, the king made offerings; priests bathed the lingam in milk and honey, lamps burned through the night, and the oduvars sang the Tevaram hymns. The Cholas asked the god for victory, and their fleets reached Srivijaya.",
  "dravidians:rain": "Tamil Nadu does not get its main rains from the summer monsoon but from the northeast monsoon of October and November. Through the long dry summer the farmers watch their tanks, the eri, shrink to cracked mud. When the rains finally come, the tanks fill, the rice is sown, and the old song goes up: vaan sirappu, the excellence of rain, as Tiruvalluvar wrote: without rain, even the sea would fail.",
  "dravidians:flood": "Aadi Perukku: in the Tamil month of Aadi, the Kaveri swells with monsoon water from the western hills and floods down through the Chola delta. On the eighteenth day, families gather on its banks to thank the river, offering rice, flowers, and yellow threads, and newly married women float lamps on the water. The Kallanai, the Grand Anicut built by Karikala Chola around the 2nd century, still turns this flood into fields.",
  "dravidians:wind": "Imagined, after the old story: Tamil tradition remembers the kadal kol, the swallowing by the sea, when a great flood took the land of the first Sangam far to the south. Fishing villages on the Coromandel coast still fear the storms that come in from the Bay of Bengal each November. The women wait on the shore for the catamarans; the men return soaked, laughing, or not at all.",
  "dravidians:fire": "Theemithi, the fire walk: in villages of Tamil Nadu, devotees of the goddess Draupadi Amman walk barefoot across a long pit of glowing coals, after weeks of fasting and the recitation of the Mahabharata. Drums beat, the crowd shouts, and the walkers go one by one, faces calm. The fire honours Draupadi's proof of her purity. To walk it unhurt is a sign that the goddess has heard the vow.",
  "dravidians:thunder": "Imagined: in the Kerala monsoon, thunderstorms come down from the Western Ghats in the evenings, shaking the coconut palms. Grandmothers in the tharavad house light the nilavilakku lamp and chant Arjuna's ten names, believed to ward off lightning. Children are hushed and pulled away from the windows. Then the rain pours on the tiled roofs and the old women go back to their stories.",
  "dravidians:birth": "Imagined: in a Tamil house, a pregnant woman goes home to her mother's family for the valaikaapu ceremony, where her arms are filled with glass bangles whose jingling is said to soothe the unborn child. When labour comes, the women of the family surround her. The newborn's name will often honour a grandparent; whether the child will be a farmer, a poet like Avvaiyar, or a soldier, only time can tell.",
  "dravidians:death": "Imagined: in a South Indian village, the paraiyar drummers lead the funeral procession, the thudumbu and parai beating loud and fast through the streets, for the drum was believed to open the way for the soul. Women beat their breasts and sing oppari laments praising the dead. Among some castes the body is buried seated; among others it is burned. On the sixteenth day, the family eats together again.",

  // ── Gurjaras ──
  "gurjaras:hunt": "The Gurjara-Pratiharas, who ruled from Kannauj in the 9th century, were described by the Arab traveller Sulaiman as having the finest cavalry in India. Their princes trained for war through the hunt, riding down boar and antelope across the dry plains of Rajasthan and Gujarat. Imagined: a young prince, spear raised, rides past thorny khejri trees after a wild boar, his father watching to see if he flinches.",
  "gurjaras:battle": "Around 738 CE, Arab armies from Sindh pressed east into Gujarat and Rajasthan. The Gurjara-Pratihara king Nagabhata I and allied rulers threw them back, and for nearly three centuries the Pratiharas held the western frontier. Arab chroniclers called their king the greatest foe of Islam in India. The Gurjaras saw themselves as the guardians of Bharat's gate, and their inscriptions boasted of it.",
  "gurjaras:gather": "Imagined: in the Thar, Gurjar herders roam with their buffalo, cows and camels. In the dry season the women gather ker berries and sangri pods from the khejri tree, sun-drying them for the year, and gum from the trees. The desert gives little, but the people know its every gift. They cook ker-sangri with yogurt and spices, a dish still eaten in Rajasthan today.",
  "gurjaras:ritual": "Imagined: at the sun temple of Modhera, built by the Solanki kings of Gujarat in 1026, priests wait for the equinox, when the first rays of dawn strike the image of Surya in the inner shrine. The step-well tank before the temple is lined with a hundred small shrines. A king asks if his next campaign will succeed; the priests read the omens of the sunrise. They answer gravely, and the king bows.",
  "gurjaras:rain": "In the desert country of Rajasthan and Gujarat, every drop of rain was stored. The great stepwells, like Rani ki Vav built at Patan by the Solanki queen Udayamati around 1063, descend seven storeys to the water. Through the dry months women walk down the carved steps to draw water. When the monsoon finally arrives, the wells fill, and people climb down to see the water rising past the goddesses carved in the walls.",
  "gurjaras:flood": "Imagined: in the Rann of Kutch, the monsoon floods the salt flats so that the white desert becomes a shallow sea for months. The herders move their camels and buffalo to the higher bets, the islands. Villages wait for the water to evaporate, when salt workers return to their pans. The flood is part of the year here, not a disaster, and the old people can read it from the sky.",
  "gurjaras:wind": "Imagined: in the Thar, the loo blows in May, a hot wind from the west that dries the throat and can kill. Herders shelter their animals in the lee of dunes and wrap their faces. Women keep water in clay pots, matkas, to cool. The children are told not to go out in the midday wind. When the evening comes, the wind drops, and the whole village comes out into the cooling air.",
  "gurjaras:fire": "Imagined, after the Rajput tradition of jauhar: when the fort of Chittorgarh was besieged, as in 1303 and again later, the women, rather than be captured, entered a great fire together, led by the queen, while the men put on saffron robes and rode out to die. The stories are told to this day with pride and with horror. The fort still stands above the plain, quiet.",
  "gurjaras:thunder": "Imagined: on the Aravalli hills above Mount Abu, the monsoon thunder echoes against the granite cliffs. Below, at the Dilwara temples, built by Jain ministers of the Solanki kings, monks continue their recitation, unmoved. The villagers in the valley say the god of the mountain is speaking. The cows huddle together, and a herder boy sings to them loudly to cover the sound of the storm.",
  "gurjaras:birth": "Imagined: in a Gurjar herding family, the birth of a child is announced by the women singing at the doorway. A knife is placed by the mother's bed against evil spirits. On the sixth day, the family lights a lamp to Bemata, the goddess who writes the child's fate on its forehead. Will this child lead the herds, or ride off to serve a king? The family prays the lines are kind.",
  "gurjaras:death": "Imagined: in a Gurjar village of Rajasthan, the body is washed and carried to the cremation ground beyond the fields. A hero who died defending the cattle may be honoured with a devli, a memorial stone carved with a horseman. On certain days, his descendants pour milk on the stone and light a lamp. The dead are not gone; they stand guard over the herd forever.",

  // ── Delhi Sultanate ──
  "delhi-sultanate:hunt": "Sultan Balban, who ruled Delhi from 1266 to 1287, used great hunting expeditions as military exercise. He rode out with thousands of horsemen into the jungles around Delhi, which were also full of bandits, and cleared them as he hunted. Mongol envoys were said to be impressed. For Balban, the hunt was statecraft: a sultan must be seen to be fearless and his army always ready.",
  "delhi-sultanate:battle": "Tarain, 1192. A year after his defeat by Prithviraj Chauhan on the same field, Muhammad of Ghor returned with mounted archers. He feigned retreat, wore down the Rajput army through the day, and then charged with his reserve cavalry. Prithviraj was captured and killed. Ghor's general Qutb ud-Din Aibak stayed on in Delhi, and in 1206 founded the sultanate. For the Rajputs, Tarain became a lament; for the Turks, a beginning.",
  "delhi-sultanate:gather": "Imagined: in the markets of Delhi under Alauddin Khilji, who fixed prices around 1300, grain merchants bring wheat and rice to the government stores. Spies report any cheating, and short weight is punished harshly. Farmers bring their harvest to the city gates; women gather herbs and dried dung from the fields. Everyone eats cheaply, but nobody dares to complain aloud.",
  "delhi-sultanate:ritual": "Imagined: before a campaign, the sultan does not consult astrologers; instead he performs istikhara, two units of prayer followed by a supplication asking God to guide him to what is good. He sleeps and waits for clarity. In the mosque of Quwwat ul-Islam, beneath the Qutb Minar, the ulema read the Quran and pray for the army. The mood is grave and quiet: the outcome belongs to God alone.",
  "delhi-sultanate:rain": "Imagined: the Hauz-i-Shamsi, the great tank built by Sultan Iltutmish around 1230 near Mehrauli, lies half-dry in the summer of a late monsoon. Water-carriers, bhishtis, come with their leather bags and leave empty. People offer the prayer for rain, salat al-istisqa, in the open field. When the clouds finally break, the tank fills, and the city celebrates by the water with sweets.",
  "delhi-sultanate:flood": "Imagined: the Yamuna, swollen by monsoon, overflows the low quarters of old Delhi below the citadel. Families move to the higher ground near the Qutb with whatever they can carry. Firuz Shah Tughlaq later built canals to tame these rivers, but in the earlier years there was only waiting. The Sufis of the khanqahs open their doors to feed the displaced, as their masters taught them.",
  "delhi-sultanate:wind": "Imagined: in the hot months before the monsoon, the andhi sweeps over Delhi, a dust storm that turns noon to night. The muezzin's call is drowned by the wind. Merchants pull down the awnings in the bazaar, and horsemen shelter in the caravanserais. Some of the old minarets crack. When the wind is gone, the people sweep the dust from their courtyards and give thanks that nothing worse happened.",
  "delhi-sultanate:fire": "In 1398 Timur sacked Delhi. His troops looted and burned the city for days; tens of thousands of people were killed, and artisans were carried off to Samarkand to build his mosques. The sultan Nasir ud-Din Mahmud fled. It was said that for two months afterward not a bird moved in the city. The Delhi Sultanate survived in name, but the old splendour of the city had gone up in smoke.",
  "delhi-sultanate:thunder": "Imagined: on a monsoon night in Delhi, a storm of thunder breaks over the dargah of Nizamuddin Auliya. The qawwals, mid-song in the courtyard, pause; then the leader laughs and starts again, louder, the harmonium straining to answer the sky. Amir Khusrau, the saint's disciple, is said to have written a verse on the sound of rain. The pilgrims stay, soaked and singing.",
  "delhi-sultanate:birth": "Imagined: in a noble house of the sultanate, a son is born. The father whispers the call to prayer into his right ear, the adhan, so that the first thing he hears is the name of God. On the seventh day, the aqiqa: his head is shaved, its weight in silver given to the poor, and a goat sacrificed. Will this child become a sultan's general, or a scholar? The family prays he will be just.",
  "delhi-sultanate:death": "Imagined: when a man of the sultanate dies, he is washed, wrapped in a white kafan, and buried within the day, his face turned toward Mecca. The janaza prayer is said in the mosque courtyard. Great men were buried in domed tombs; Iltutmish's tomb near the Qutb Minar, built around 1235, is among the earliest. For the poor, a simple mound is enough. All return to God, it is said.",

  // ── Tughlaq Dynasty ──
  "tughlaq-dynasty:hunt": "Firuz Shah Tughlaq, who ruled from 1351 to 1388, loved the hunt and built hunting lodges, shikargahs, in the ridge forests around Delhi; the ruined lodge called Kushk-i-Shikar still stands on the Delhi Ridge. Imagined: the old sultan rides out at dawn with cheetahs and falcons, and his courtiers note with relief that he is in good humour; on hunting days, petitioners found him more generous.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:battle": "In the 1330s and 1340s Muhammad bin Tughlaq's empire, stretched from the Himalaya to Madurai, begins to break apart. Revolt follows revolt: in Bengal, in Ma'bar in the far south, in the Deccan where the Bahmanis rise in 1347. The sultan marches from one end of India to the other putting them down. In 1351 he dies of fever on campaign in Sindh, still chasing a rebel. The historian Badauni wrote that the sultan was freed from his people, and they from him.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:gather": "Firuz Shah Tughlaq dug canals from the Yamuna and the Sutlej to water the dry lands of Hisar, and his reign is remembered for cheap grain and orchards: some 1,200 gardens around Delhi, it was said. Imagined: villagers in Hisar who had never seen such green gather mangoes and grapes from the new orchards, and give thanks for a sultan who built rather than burned.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:ritual": "Imagined: in 1327, before moving the whole court and population of Delhi to Daulatabad in the Deccan, Muhammad bin Tughlaq prays istikhara and asks the scholars for counsel. He is learned in philosophy, astronomy and law. The scholars are cautious; some dare say nothing. The sultan rises from his prayer certain. History would judge the decision harshly, but that night, the palace was hushed and solemn, waiting on God.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:rain": "Imagined: during the great famine of the 1330s, when rains failed year after year across the Doab, Muhammad bin Tughlaq opened state granaries and gave loans to farmers to dig wells. The people of Delhi crowded the open prayer grounds for istisqa, the prayer for rain. When the clouds came at last, children ran into the lanes and old men wept. Ibn Battuta, then a judge in Delhi, saw the hunger and recorded it.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:flood": "Imagined: Firuz Shah's canals bring water to the desert, but in the year of a great monsoon the Yamuna bursts into them and floods the low villages near Hisar. Officials rush to close the sluice gates. Farmers who had begged for water now beg it to stop. In time the water recedes, leaving rich silt, and the farmers grumble and sow a double crop.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:wind": "Imagined: on the long road to Daulatabad in 1327, the people of Delhi, forced to move south by order of Muhammad bin Tughlaq, march for forty days. A dust storm hits the column in the Malwa plateau; tents fly away, the old and the sick fall behind. Ibn Battuta later heard that many died on the road. When the sultan let them return years later, few did.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:fire": "Tughlaqabad, the vast fortified city built by Ghiyas ud-Din Tughlaq in the 1320s, was soon abandoned. Legend says the Sufi saint Nizamuddin Auliya, angered that the sultan had forbidden workmen to build his stepwell, cursed it: may it be inhabited by herders, or stay desolate. The sultan died in 1325 when a pavilion collapsed on him. Shepherds later lit their fires in the empty halls.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:thunder": "Muhammad bin Tughlaq introduced copper and brass token coins in 1329 to stand for silver. Within months, every house became a mint, and forgeries flooded the markets. The sultan had to buy back the fakes with real silver, and heaps of copper coins lay outside the fort of Tughlaqabad. The news struck Delhi like thunder; merchants said that the sultan's ideas were brilliant and his people were not ready.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:birth": "Imagined: in Delhi in the 1300s, a son is born to a Tughlaq noble. The adhan is whispered in his ear and a Sufi from the khanqah of Nizamuddin is asked to bless him. Some in the family hope he will be a great general like Ghiyas ud-Din; others whisper that the times are dangerous and the sultan's favour fickle. The mother only hopes he will live through his first summer.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:death": "Ghiyas ud-Din Tughlaq lies in a red sandstone tomb, built in 1325, that once stood in an artificial lake connected to his fort at Tughlaqabad. Imagined: on the day of his burial, the people of Delhi gather, the janaza prayer is said, and the body is laid facing Mecca. Some mourn; some whisper about the collapsed pavilion and the saint's curse. His son, Muhammad, is now sultan.",

  // ── Burmese ──
  "burmese:hunt": "Imagined: in the teak forests of Upper Burma, the royal elephant catchers of the Konbaung kings drive wild elephants into a stockade, the kheddah, with drums and shouts. A white elephant, if one is ever found, is a sign of a righteous king and is received with festivals at the court of Ava or Mandalay. The catchers themselves are proud men, risking their lives for the glory of the throne.",
  "burmese:battle": "In 1767 the army of King Hsinbyushin besieged Ayutthaya, the Siamese capital, for over a year and finally stormed and burned it. The Burmese carried away thousands of captives, including artists and musicians who brought Siamese dance and music to the Burmese court. In Burma the victory was celebrated; in Thailand it is remembered as a catastrophe. Within months, the Chinese invaded Burma from the north, and the army had to rush home.",
  "burmese:gather": "Imagined: in the Irrawaddy valley, women gather thanaka bark, grinding it on flat stones with water into a pale yellow paste they smear on their cheeks against the sun. On the river islands, families pick tamarind and beans as the waters fall. They carry their harvest in baskets slung from shoulder poles to the market, smiling under their thanaka.",
  "burmese:ritual": "Imagined: at the Shwedagon pagoda in Yangon, a family asks a fortune-teller, a bedin saya, about a son's future. He consults the boy's birth day, for each day of the week has its own planet and animal, and his planetary post at the pagoda. The family pours water over the Buddha image at the corner of their day and lights candles. Gongs and bells sound across the platform. They leave hopeful.",
  "burmese:rain": "Thingyan, the water festival of April, marks the Burmese New Year, in the hottest month just before the monsoon. For days, the whole country throws water at each other from buckets, hoses and silver bowls, laughing. It is meant to wash away the sins of the old year. The rains themselves follow soon, and the farmers plough their fields, already wet with festival joy.",
  "burmese:flood": "Imagined: on the Irrawaddy delta, the monsoon swells the river until the paddies vanish under a brown sea. Villages on stilts become islands; children paddle to school in boats. The fishermen are busy and happy: the flood brings fish into the fields. The old monk in the village monastery says the river has always done this and always will.",
  "burmese:wind": "On 2 May 2008 Cyclone Nargis struck the Irrawaddy delta with a storm surge that swept over the low villages. Around 140,000 people died. The military government at first blocked foreign aid, and local monks and volunteers rowed out to rescue the survivors themselves. The delta remembers both the grief and the anger, and the small boats that came out anyway.",
  "burmese:fire": "Tazaungdaing, the festival of lights, falls on the full moon of the eighth Burmese month in November, at the end of the rainy season. Hot-air balloons of paper are launched into the night sky, especially at Taunggyi in the Shan hills, some carrying fireworks that burst high overhead. Crowds cheer and duck when one misfires. Monks are given robes woven overnight in weaving contests. Fire here is joy.",
  "burmese:thunder": "Imagined: in Burmese tradition, the beginning of the rains is marked by Waso, when monks begin their three-month retreat. The first thunderstorms crack over the plains around Bagan, lighting up its thousands of temples. Villagers say the nats, the spirits, are restless. They offer coconuts and bananas at the spirit house and wait for the rain to settle the dust.",
  "burmese:birth": "Imagined: in a Burmese village, a baby is born, and the family notes the day of the week, for the child's name will begin with a letter belonging to that day. An astrologer is consulted for the naming ceremony. A Monday child is said to be jealous, a Thursday child mild. The grandparents wonder whether this child will one day become a monk, a farmer, or something no one has imagined.",
  "burmese:death": "In Burma the dead are given a funeral that is more about merit than mourning. The body is washed and dressed, and a coin is placed in the mouth to pay the ferryman. Monks chant the Abhidhamma, and the family offers food to the monks for seven days so the dead may be reborn well. When a revered monk dies, his body may be lain in state for a year, then cremated in a grand ceremony with rockets.",

  // ── Khmer ──
  "khmer:hunt": "Imagined, after the reliefs of Angkor and the Chinese envoy Zhou Daguan, who lived there in 1296: in the forests around Angkor, men catch wild elephants and rhinoceros, and trap kingfishers whose feathers are sold to Chinese merchants. The king rides out on an elephant under a golden parasol. Hunters bring back deer and boar, but the people of Angkor ate mostly fish and rice.",
  "khmer:battle": "In 1177 a Cham fleet sailed up the Mekong and into the Tonle Sap and sacked Angkor. The Khmer prince who became Jayavarman VII drove them out in a great naval battle on the lake, carved on the walls of the Bayon with boats, drowned warriors and crocodiles. He went on to build the walled city of Angkor Thom and more hospitals than any king before him. The Khmer remember him as a healer-king.",
  "khmer:gather": "Imagined: at the edges of the Tonle Sap, as the water falls in the dry season, families gather fish trapped in the shallows, and women collect lotus roots and water lilies. The fish are salted and pounded into prahok, the fermented paste that flavours nearly every Khmer meal. In the evening, the smell of drying fish hangs over the villages, and people sing as they work.",
  "khmer:ritual": "Imagined: at Angkor Wat, the brahmins of the king's court perform a rite before the rains, chanting Sanskrit verses and offering rice, flowers and fire to Vishnu. The king asks the court astrologers, the hora, whether the year will bring good harvests. The answer is given in sacred terms. In the Royal Ploughing Ceremony still held today, the royal oxen choose among bowls of rice, beans and water to predict the season.",
  "khmer:rain": "Imagined: in the dry months, the moat of Angkor Wat shrinks and the rice fields crack. The Khmer kings built the great barays, reservoirs like the West Baray, eight kilometres long, to hold water. When the monsoon finally arrives in May, the barays fill, the rice is sown, and the people go to the temples to give thanks with offerings of fruit and flowers. The whole land breathes again.",
  "khmer:flood": "Each monsoon the Mekong rises so high that the Tonle Sap River reverses its flow, pushing water back up into the great lake, which swells to several times its size and floods the forest. In November, when the river turns back again, the Khmer celebrate Bon Om Touk, the water festival, with long-boat races in Phnom Penh, illuminated floats and fireworks. The flood is life here.",
  "khmer:wind": "Imagined: a typhoon blowing in from the South China Sea crosses the Annamite mountains and reaches the plains of Cambodia as a violent storm. The wooden houses of Angkor, unlike the stone temples, are shaken and some blown down. The people shelter in the temples' galleries. In the morning, the stones are unmoved, and the people rebuild their houses quickly, as they always have.",
  "khmer:fire": "Imagined: at Angkor, the cremation of a king was a vast spectacle, the pyre built high in the royal square before the Terrace of the Elephants. In the dry season, farmers also set fire to the stubble of the rice fields; at night, the plain around Angkor glowed red. The fire cleared the fields for new crops, and the ash fertilized the soil.",
  "khmer:thunder": "Imagined: at the beginning of the monsoon, thunder cracks over the towers of the Bayon, where two hundred stone faces of the bodhisattva smile in every direction. The people in the city beneath say the naga, the serpent spirit of the waters, is rising. Children run to the moats to watch the first rain hit the water. The farmers smile: the thunder means the season has turned.",
  "khmer:birth": "Imagined: in an Angkorian house on stilts, a woman gives birth beside a fire, where she will lie for days to dry and warm her body, a custom Zhou Daguan noted in 1296. The midwife ties a red string around the baby's wrist. Monks bless the family. Will this child carve the stone faces of the Bayon, or plough the king's fields, or rule? Only the gods know.",
  "khmer:death": "Zhou Daguan, the Chinese envoy who lived at Angkor in 1296, wrote that the Khmer often left their dead in the open fields for vultures and dogs, and thought it a good sign if the body was taken quickly. Others were cremated. Today, Khmer Buddhist funerals have monks chanting, the body cremated at a pagoda, and the ashes kept in a stupa. Pchum Ben, each autumn, feeds the hungry ghosts of the ancestors.",

  // ── Malay ──
  "malay:hunt": "Imagined: in the forests of the Malay Peninsula, a pawang, the village shaman-hunter, leads men after wild boar and mouse-deer, the kancil, which in Malay tales outwits the tiger and the crocodile. Before entering, he asks permission of the forest spirits with a jampi, a spoken charm. Tigers are not hunted lightly; people call the tiger Datuk, grandfather, to avoid offending it.",
  "malay:battle": "In 1511 a Portuguese fleet under Afonso de Albuquerque attacked Melaka, the richest port of Southeast Asia. Sultan Mahmud Shah's men fought from the bridge over the river and with war elephants, but after weeks the city fell. The sultan retreated and his son founded the sultanate of Johor. Malay tradition remembers Hang Tuah, the legendary laksamana of Melaka, who said: Takkan Melayu hilang di dunia, the Malays will never vanish from the earth.",
  "malay:gather": "Imagined: on the coasts of the Straits of Melaka, Orang Laut, the sea people, gather sea cucumbers, pearl shells and turtle eggs from the reefs, and the camphor and gaharu wood that the forest people trade down the rivers. Merchants from China, India and Arabia wait in Melaka's harbour for the monsoon wind to turn. The gatherers know the price of each thing and bargain hard.",
  "malay:ritual": "Imagined: in a Malay village in the sultanate of Melaka, before a long voyage, the captain asks the imam to pray istikhara with him, and they hold a kenduri, a communal feast with prayers. An older bomoh may still quietly offer rice to the spirits of the sea. Islam and older custom sit side by side. The village wishes him well; he sails at the turn of the wind.",
  "malay:rain": "In the Malay world, life turns on the monsoon winds: the northeast monsoon from November brings heavy rain to the east coast of the Peninsula, and the southwest monsoon carries ships from India. Imagined: in Kelantan, after the dry spell, the first heavy rains of the musim tengkujuh arrive, the fishermen haul their boats onto the beach, and families gather at home to eat and wait for months of rain.",
  "malay:flood": "Every year the northeast monsoon floods the rivers of Kelantan, Terengganu and Pahang, and villagers call it bah. In December 2014 the bah kuning, the yellow flood, was the worst in memory, forcing hundreds of thousands from their homes. Malay houses on stilts, rumah panggung, were built for this. Neighbours share food from mosque kitchens, and when the water falls, everyone helps scrub the mud out.",
  "malay:wind": "Imagined: a storm called the Sumatras blows out of the west across the Straits of Melaka before dawn, a squall line of wind and lightning feared by sailors. A trading prahu caught in it must ride out the gale under bare masts. Its crew pray, and recite the azan to calm the wind. By sunrise the sea is flat again, and they laugh and shake the water from their sarongs.",
  "malay:fire": "Imagined: in the early days of the Sultanate of Melaka, the houses of palm thatch and timber were close together, and fire spread quickly. The town was rebuilt many times. In the dry season, villagers clear land for rice by burning, the tebas-tebang, and the smoke hangs over the river valleys. Fire is a tool and a danger, and the people respect both.",
  "malay:thunder": "Imagined: in Malay belief, thunder and lightning are signs of the power of God, and a strong storm is a time to recite prayers. Children are told not to point at lightning, and mirrors are covered. When the thunder rolls over the rice fields, the old farmers smile: the rains will be good and the padi will grow tall. The fear and the hope come together.",
  "malay:birth": "Imagined: in a Malay kampung, a woman gives birth with the help of a mak bidan, the midwife. The father recites the azan in the baby's right ear. Then comes the berpantang, forty-four days of rest for the mother with herbal baths and warmth. At the naming, the cukur jambul, a lock of the baby's hair is cut while the family sings praises of the Prophet. What will this child bring?",
  "malay:death": "When a Malay Muslim dies, the body is washed, wrapped in a white kain kapan and buried the same day if possible, facing Mecca. Men carry the bier to the mosque for the solat jenazah, then to the graveyard, where the imam recites the talqin, reminding the dead of the answers to give the angels. Neighbours bring food. On the third, seventh and hundredth days, tahlil prayers are said.",

  // ── Vietnamese ──
  "vietnamese:hunt": "Imagined: in the mountains of northern Vietnam, a Vietnamese hunter and his Mường guide track deer and wild boar with crossbows, the weapon the Lạc Việt legend says the turtle god gave to King An Dương Vương. They leave an offering of rice and wine at the edge of the forest for the mountain spirit, Sơn Tinh. Tigers are feared and respected, called Ông, grandfather.",
  "vietnamese:battle": "Bạch Đằng River, 1288. The Mongol fleet of Kublai Khan, retreating down the river, met the trap prepared by Trần Hưng Đạo: iron-tipped stakes driven into the riverbed, hidden by the high tide. The Vietnamese lured the fleet in, the tide fell, the stakes pierced the hulls, and the Mongol ships were destroyed. It was the same trick Ngô Quyền had used against the Southern Han in 938. The river is still a symbol of resistance.",
  "vietnamese:gather": "Imagined: in the Red River delta after the harvest, women and children wade the paddies gathering crabs, snails and small fish trapped in the mud, and pick water spinach from the canals. In the late afternoon, they carry their baskets home on bamboo poles, singing quan họ, the call-and-response folk songs of Bắc Ninh. The rice harvest is in; the mood is easy.",
  "vietnamese:ritual": "Imagined: on the eve of Tết, the lunar new year, a family in Hà Nội sets out offerings on the ancestral altar: a five-fruit tray, sticky rice cakes, bánh chưng, incense and paper gold. They ask the ancestors for guidance and a good year. On the first morning, someone of good fortune is chosen to be the first to step through the door, the xông đất. At the temple, people draw fortune slips.",
  "vietnamese:rain": "Imagined: after the dry season, the farmers of the Mekong delta watch for the southwest monsoon of May. When the first rains come, the dust turns to mud, the canals fill, and the families rush out to plant. Children splash in the rain with banana leaves on their heads. The grandmother says that the rain is the gift of heaven and that the rice will grow tall this year.",
  "vietnamese:flood": "The legend of Sơn Tinh and Thủy Tinh: the Mountain Spirit and the Water Spirit both sought the hand of the daughter of the Hùng King. Sơn Tinh arrived first with his gifts and won her, and the furious Water Spirit raised the floods, but the Mountain Spirit raised the hills higher. Every year, the Vietnamese say, Thủy Tinh still attacks with the monsoon floods of the Red River, and every year he is beaten back by the dikes.",
  "vietnamese:wind": "Typhoons, bão, strike the central coast of Vietnam almost every autumn. Imagined: in a fishing village near Huế, the men drag their basket boats, thúng chai, up the beach and tie down their roofs with ropes and stones. The family huddles together while the wind howls. In the morning, they walk out to see what is left, and begin, together, to rebuild.",
  "vietnamese:fire": "Imagined: in a village of the Red River delta, a family burns paper offerings, vàng mã, gold and silver paper money, paper clothes and houses, for their ancestors on the fifteenth day of the seventh lunar month, the Vu Lan festival. The fire carries the offerings to the other world. Children watch the ash float up, and the parents tell them stories of the grandparents they never met.",
  "vietnamese:thunder": "Imagined: in Vietnamese folk belief, thunder is Thiên Lôi, the god of thunder, who strikes down wrongdoers with his axe. When the storm rumbles over the Red River delta, children who misbehaved are told that Thiên Lôi is watching. The adults laugh, but they still burn incense when the thunder is very loud. After the storm, the rice fields shine green.",
  "vietnamese:birth": "Imagined: in a Vietnamese family, the child is a link in a long line of ancestors. A month after birth, the family holds the đầy tháng, a ceremony to thank the twelve midwife goddesses, bà mụ, who taught the baby to smile and suck. Offerings are made and the child is formally introduced to the ancestors. Will this child be another Trần Hưng Đạo, or simply a good son? They pray for both.",
  "vietnamese:death": "In a Vietnamese funeral, the family dresses in white mourning headbands. The body is laid in a coffin at home, with the ancestor altar, incense and a photograph. Monks or a master of ceremonies chant, and a funeral band of drums and the kèn plays. The family walks behind the coffin to the grave. Afterward, the dead joins the ancestors, honoured at the altar every year on the day of death, the giỗ.",

  // ── Lac Viet ──
  "lac-viet:hunt": "Imagined, after the Đông Sơn drums: around 500 BCE in the Red River valley, Lạc Việt hunters in feathered headdresses pursue deer and wild birds with spears and crossbows. Their bronze drums show such men, and herons and deer in rings around a central sun. The hunters tattoo their bodies with water creatures, as Chinese chroniclers later noted, so the river dragons will spare them.",
  "lac-viet:battle": "In 40 CE the Trưng sisters, Trưng Trắc and Trưng Nhị, raised the Lạc lords against the Chinese Han governor in the Red River delta, after Trưng Trắc's husband was executed. Riding elephants at the head of their armies, they took sixty-five citadels and ruled for three years. In 43 CE the Han general Ma Yuan crushed the revolt, and the sisters are said to have drowned themselves in the Hát River rather than surrender. Vietnam honours them still.",
  "lac-viet:gather": "Imagined: in the delta of the Red River, around 300 BCE, Lạc Việt women gather shellfish and snails from the marshes and harvest wild rice. Their villages stand on stilts. Men catch fish with bamboo traps. In the evenings, the people gather beneath the drum house to eat and to sing. The land is wet and generous, and they are content.",
  "lac-viet:ritual": "Imagined, after the Đông Sơn bronze drums: the Lạc lords beat the great drums in the rain-calling ritual, the frogs cast on the drum's rim signalling their power over rain. Priests in feathered costumes dance to the drumbeat, and boats carry warriors in a procession on the river. They ask the spirits whether the year will be kind. The thunder of the drum echoes over the paddies.",
  "lac-viet:rain": "Imagined: in the hot season before the rains, the Lạc Việt beat their bronze drums, whose rims bear little frogs, the heralds of rain. The villagers wait for the clouds over the mountains of the north. When the first monsoon storm comes in, they shout and dance, and the drums answer the thunder. The rice planting begins with joy, as it has for centuries.",
  "lac-viet:flood": "The legend of Lạc Long Quân, the Dragon Lord of the waters, and Âu Cơ, the mountain fairy, whose hundred sons were born from a hundred eggs, gave the Việt their origin. Fifty went to the sea with their father, fifty to the mountains with their mother. Imagined: when the Red River floods, the Lạc people say the dragon father is visiting, and they move to higher ground until he returns to the sea.",
  "lac-viet:wind": "Imagined: in the delta of the Red River, around 200 BCE, a great storm comes in off the Gulf of Tonkin. The stilt houses shake and the canoes are dragged up the riverbank. The Lạc people shelter beneath the floor of the communal house, where the great bronze drum is kept. The storm passes; the drum is unharmed, and they take it as a good omen.",
  "lac-viet:fire": "Imagined: in the Đông Sơn villages around 300 BCE, bronze smiths pour molten metal into clay moulds to cast the great drums. The whole village gathers to watch. If the casting fails, it is a bad omen; if it succeeds, the drum is beaten for the first time, and the village celebrates for days. The smiths are honoured as masters of fire and earth.",
  "lac-viet:thunder": "Imagined: the Lạc Việt believed that their bronze drums could summon rain and thunder. When the sky darkens over the Red River valley, the lord orders the great drum beaten, and the people say the drum and the thunder are speaking to each other. The children hide; the old men nod. In the legends of the Hùng Kings, the thunder god himself protects the land.",
  "lac-viet:birth": "Imagined, after the legend: the Việt trace their origin to a hundred eggs laid by Âu Cơ, from which a hundred sons hatched, the eldest becoming the first Hùng King of Văn Lang. In a Lạc village, every birth recalls that story. The midwife washes the newborn in river water; the father ties a thread to its wrist. Will this child be a lord or a farmer? The ancestors will decide.",
  "lac-viet:death": "Imagined, after finds at Việt Khê and other Đông Sơn sites: a Lạc lord is laid in a boat-shaped coffin hollowed from a tree trunk, with bronze drums, weapons and jars for the journey. The coffin is buried beneath a mound by the river. Mourners in feathered headdresses dance and beat drums, as on the drum faces. The boat carries the dead to the land of the ancestors.",
});
